    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Always name the shape first"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "const rows = grid.length;\nconst cols = grid[0].length;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Write these two lines before anything else, every time. Most grid bugs are `rows` and `cols` swapped somewhere in the middle of a loop, and naming them up front makes the mistake visible. Remember the order: `grid[r][c]` is **row first, then column** — like reading a book, you pick the line before the word."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Boundaries as four variables"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "For traversals that work inward, track the live region explicitly rather than computing it:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "let top = 0;\nlet bottom = rows - 1;\nlet left = 0;\nlet right = cols - 1;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Shrinking a boundary after finishing an edge is far easier to reason about than deriving offsets from a ring number. **Spiral order traversal** is built entirely on this."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Direction vectors"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "For anything that steps between neighbours, encode the moves as data instead of writing four near-identical blocks:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "const DIRECTIONS = [[-1, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0], [0, -1]];   // up, right, down, left\n\nfor (const [dr, dc] of DIRECTIONS) {\n  const nr = r + dr;\n  const nc = c + dc;\n  if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols) {\n    // visit grid[nr][nc]\n  }\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "One bounds check instead of four copies. Rotating through directions in order also becomes a modulo away, which is exactly what spiral traversal needs."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Check bounds before reading"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The single most common grid crash is reading `grid[nr][nc]` before confirming `nr` and `nc` are valid. In JavaScript a bad row gives `undefined`, and indexing that throws — often far from the real mistake. In Java and C++ it's an out-of-bounds error or silent garbage."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "What this chapter covers"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "These are the 2D array problems interviewers ask most, from easy to medium. Each has a picture, a dry-run simulator you can feed your own grid, and code in four languages."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Transpose a matrix** — rows become columns; the simplest index swap.",
        "**Pascal's triangle** — building a jagged 2D array row by row from the row above.",
        "**Rotate a matrix in place** — composing two simple transforms.",
        "**Spiral order** — shrinking boundaries.",
        "**Set matrix zeroes** — using the grid as its own storage.",
        "**Search a sorted 2D matrix** — treating a grid as one long sorted list.",
        "**Diagonal traversal** — noticing an invariant."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Each is a technique that reappears constantly, not a one-off puzzle."
    }
  ],
